WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.252 --> 00:00:02.919
(vibrant music)

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:09.492
(squeaking)

00:00:09.492 --> 00:00:12.075
(lively music)

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.300
(Frank laughing) - Very nice.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.133
Hey, what's up?

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:18.660
My name is Frank. I'm a
New York City firefighter.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.620
I work in Ladder 10,

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.500
and I'm a Ladder 10 truck chauffeur.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.160
- What's up, man? My name is Ahmed Clark,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.750
I am a seven-year veteran of
the Trenton Fire Department.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:26.860
- I'm Michael Mianco.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.670
I'm lieutenant in Hook and Ladder Company

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.580
for the city of Glen Cove
Volunteer Fire Department.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.060
- [Ahmed] Any time that
there's any kind of emergency

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.320
we're typically the
first people who respond.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:36.930
A large part of our job is

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.510
community involvement and engagement.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.320
Just being present, making
real connections with people.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.460
- It's the best job in the world

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.750
because helping people is, like,

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.970
one of the biggest benefits
you could always, like, have.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.980
- You know, being a fireman
is like my second family.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.330
They're all about taking
care of each other,

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:51.340
and, you know, it's a brotherhood.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.757
Like, we live together, we eat
together, we sleep together.

00:00:53.757 --> 00:00:55.210
- We take care of one another

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.959
and the things that we have as a unit.

00:00:57.959 --> 00:01:00.542
(upbeat music)

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.010
- I have three commitments to my people.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.690
- Everybody who clocks
in will also clock out.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.196
Everybody gets a fair shake,

00:01:08.196 --> 00:01:11.220
and no one, and I mean no one,

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.109
gets left behind when
it comes to skin care.

00:01:14.109 --> 00:01:15.070
(chuckling)

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.470
- I get facials on the outside,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:17.417
and I'm not telling the firemen that.

00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:18.960
I'd get crushed in the firehouse.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.340
So, I mean the least you tell
them the better off you are

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.360
so we're definitely not having
skin-care conversations.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.660
- So I think the interaction
was pretty authentic.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.860
The scene looks like a hotel resort.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:29.693
I don't know what firehouse that was.

00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:31.860
- I don't know, that's not my firehouse.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.513
- Definitely not mine.
- (laughing)

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.450
- Hey, everybody, I'm Rob
Lowe from "9-1-1: Lone Star."

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.050
I want to thank you
all for all that you do

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.170
for our communities, and as a reward

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.990
I'm hoping that you enjoy your spa day.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:54.310
God bless. Use it in health,
safety, and in happiness.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.143
- That's actually really cool.

00:01:55.143 --> 00:01:56.086
- I'm all in.

00:01:56.086 --> 00:01:57.327
I don't know about him.

00:01:57.327 --> 00:01:58.160
(Frank laughs heartily)
- I never been to one.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.405
- It's gonna be quite an experience.

00:01:59.405 --> 00:02:00.238
- It's gonna be a different
experience for me.

00:02:00.238 --> 00:02:01.610
I never set foot in one.

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:02.443
- I'm only gonna need an hour,

00:02:02.443 --> 00:02:03.749
he's gonna need about 12.

00:02:03.749 --> 00:02:05.343
- [Michael] I'm gonna need a whole day.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:09.406
(big band music)

00:02:09.406 --> 00:02:11.477
- [Ahmed] We head downtown
to the Four Seasons.

00:02:11.477 --> 00:02:12.869
- [Female Receptionist]
Welcome to the spa here at

00:02:12.869 --> 00:02:15.360
Four Seasons near downtown.
- Thank you.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.717
- I just have some wellness juices

00:02:16.717 --> 00:02:19.020
to get you gentlemen started.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.320
- [Ahmed] They greeted us well.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.760
We were escorted upstairs to the spa area.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.950
- I think these rooms
make me want to floss.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.130
- Do they? (laughing)

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.827
- [Ahmed] Just jumped into
a whole different world.

00:02:28.827 --> 00:02:30.520
(big band music)

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:31.520
- [Frank] We come into the lounge,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.790
and we have these nice
juicy cheese burgers

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.510
with onions on top of it
and the nice French fries.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.560
And, once you see that on the plate,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.690
you know you're in for a great
treat the rest of the day.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.350
I gotta pedicure, and it was amazing.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.110
She filed down my nails
and gave me a foot massage.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.060
It was completely relaxing.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.560
- [Michael] Like, this is my
first time ever having, like,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.620
a real professional massage.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.570
I'm feeling just amazing right now.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.350
The hot rocks, the oils,
the smells, everything.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.183
It was great.

00:02:58.183 --> 00:03:00.203
It really made me feel so much better.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.730
- [Ahmed] I think the
massage was life-changing.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:07.640
She began to do an
oil-infused scalp massage,

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.301
which is something I
never experienced before.

00:03:10.301 --> 00:03:11.370
- [Frank] You know, I
wasn't born a firemen.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.200
You know, you learn how
to be a fireman, you know.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.400
But, you know, for anybody
it's still scary, you know,

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.233
I mean it's real life, you know.

00:03:16.233 --> 00:03:18.830
And then you're home, and you
think about your children,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.389
and you want to make it
home at the end of the night

00:03:20.389 --> 00:03:22.587
but also you want to
do your job, you know,

00:03:22.587 --> 00:03:23.822
and your job is to protect

00:03:23.822 --> 00:03:25.810
the people of the city of New York.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.530
- [Ahmed] The fire service, in general,

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.550
is no longer just guys
running around with axes.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.650
We literally do almost everything

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.440
as far as emergencies are concerned.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.250
I'm a hazardous materials
tech, I am a rescue diver.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.450
We also are an emergency responder.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.520
It is definitely a very stressful job

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.410
ff you don't have the proper outlets.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.080
- [Michael] A lot of us
on our jobs, you know,

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.710
we forget about ourselves,

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:48.880
and you know, taking care of ourselves

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.270
probably should, maybe, almost come first.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.630
'Cause if we don't take care of ourselves,

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:55.050
how we gonna go out and
protect other people?

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.640
- [Frank] To me, like, when
you feel good about yourself,

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.170
you look good, you feel
good, you just perform better

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:00.780
in anything you do in life, you know.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:01.620
- [Michael] Running around,

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.410
never really having time for yourself

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.070
'cause you're always
thinking about somebody else.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:08.670
You know, it really is nice to
have something done for you.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:11.110
Taking care of yourself,
pampering yourself a little bit.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:12.860
- So, thanks, Rob Lowe,
thanks "9-1-1: Lone Star"

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.150
for this great opportunity.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.410
- Captain, appreciate it.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:16.730
- My favorite part today

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:17.563
was getting to know both
of you guys and what you do

00:04:17.563 --> 00:04:19.902
- [Ahmed] Aww, that's nice.
- in the fire service...

00:04:19.902 --> 00:04:21.070
- [Ahmed] Aww, that's so nice.
- ...in New Jersey

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.520
and what you do in the fire
service on Long Island.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.780
- [Michael] Honestly, I'm really blessed

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.950
to meet every one of these guys.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.490
They're honestly real good people,

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.080
and as silly as it
sounds, they are heroes.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.830
- Don't miss the two-night
premiere of "9-1-1: Lone Star."

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.830
- Sunday, January 19.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:35.913
- Only on Fox.

00:04:37.026 --> 00:04:39.943
(light rock music)

00:04:51.892 --> 00:04:54.225
(squeaking)

